  "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for $x$ kids attending, the summer camp has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=300\\cdot x=300x\n$$\n\nand a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nC(x)={{2\\,000}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}\\,\\,+{{100x.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =300x-(2\\,000+100x) \\\\  & =200x-2\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 200.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=200$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<200.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=200.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ has no solutions.\n\nThus, our candidates are $x=0$ and $x=200.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =200(0)-2\\,000=-2\\,000 \\\\ P(200) & =200(200)-2\\,000=38\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the summer camp will have a maximum profit level when $200$ kids attend.",
